{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q021_nad_binding_convergent_evolution","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, NAD(P) binding is not confined to one homology group. One explicit example is fold c.2, SUNID 51734, “NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains,” which contains superfamily c.2.1, SUNID 51735, of the same name; a separate fold, c.3, SUNID 51904, is “FAD/NAD(P)-binding domain” and contains superfamily c.3.1, SUNID 51905. SCOPe marks c.2.1 and c.3.1 as superfamily-level nodes whose members have homology implied within each superfamily, while the parent fold nodes are above the homology-bearing level and their homology responses explicitly warn that SCOPe levels above superfamily do not imply homology. Thus NAD(P)-binding enzymes can include divergent relatives within a SCOPe superfamily, but the recurrence of NAD(P)-binding in different folds or different superfamilies is not, by itself, a SCOPe assertion that all such enzymes are homologous.

The evolutionary conclusion has to be asymmetric. Multiple NAD(P)-binding folds are compatible with convergent recruitment of a chemically useful cofactor-binding solution on distinct scaffolds, and SCOPe even annotates the c.2 Rossmann fold with the caveat that its nucleotide-binding mode is similar to the next two folds/superfamilies. However, similarity in cofactor-binding mode or in catalytic-site geometry is functional/structural evidence, not the hierarchy level at which SCOPe asserts common ancestry. Shared family would support the closest SCOPe relationship; shared superfamily would support probable common ancestry despite possible low sequence identity or functional divergence; shared fold alone would support a common topology; shared class alone would support only broad secondary-structure organization.

Consequently, transferring homology, whole-enzyme function, exact substrate specificity, reaction chemistry, stereochemistry, oligomeric state, or a whole-domain template only because two enzymes both bind NAD, NADP, or place residues similarly around the cofactor would be unsafe. A stronger claim that two NAD(P)-binding sites are homologous across superfamilies would need external evidence such as a global structural alignment with equivalent core topology, sequence or motif correspondence in the same structural context, phylogenetic or literature support, and ligand-bound/biochemical data for the cofactor and reaction. A stronger claim of independent origins or convergence likewise cannot be proven merely from “different SCOPe superfamilies”; the classification only says SCOPe does not make a positive superfamily-level homology assertion between those nodes.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","MCP:get_scop_node","MCP:list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.3","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.3/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.3/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.3.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.3.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51904/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51904/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51905/homology"]}]}
